tion. RESULTS: As of January 31, 2006, 481 cases were reviewed for case
assignment. Five were returned for additional abstraction and 3 were not
DBMD. The remaining 474 cases were assigned as follows: 349 (73%) defi-
nite, 56 (12%) probable, 52 (11%) possible, 12 (3%) asymptomatic and 4
(1%) female. All reviewers agreed on initial review in 66% of the cases, the
remainder went to conference call. On blinded re-review, 92% of cases were
assigned the same diagnostic category. Diagnosis is dependent on muscle
biopsy in ~25%. CONCLUSIONS/RELEVANCE: MDSTARnet is a
unique population-based dataset. Its value is dependent on accuracy of
DBMD case classification. The MDSTARnet case definitions and the review
appronch allow a high degree of confidence in the results. It is of interest
that approximately 25% of patients lack diagnostic genetic information.
